One test does all of it -- gemini's `test_trailing_comment_text_on_marker_line`:

    "<!-- fb:creates foo.rs -->  comment\n"   ->  is this a declaration?

gemini and or-nemotron say yes. glm and codex-luna say no. My spec pinned clause 6 (a marker
counts only at the start of a line) and said NOTHING about what may follow the closing `-->`.
Two readings, both faithful.

THE CRITICS FOUND THE SAME HOLE THREE MORE TIMES, INDEPENDENTLY. None of them saw the matrix.

  gemini on glm: "`parse_declaration` extracts the path via `rest.strip_suffix("-->")?.trim()`
    ... If a prompt contains multiple declarations or trailing comments on one line ...
    `strip_suffix` matches the trailing `-->`, capturing the intermediate markup into the path
    ... an unpinned boundary rather than a defect."

  glm on or-nemotron: "the parser accepts `<!-- fb:\tcreates a.rs -->` and
    `<!-- fb: creates a.rs -->` ... yet rejects `creates\ta.rs`. Under a strict single-space
    grammar the acceptance is the defect; under a flexible grammar the rejection is.
    Whichever reading the spec takes, exactly one of these two behaviours is wrong."

  or-nemotron on codex-luna: "rejects valid HTML comments like `<!--fb:creates path-->`
    (no space after opener) ... The author acknowledges this as intentional narrowness."

Four separate findings, one cause: I pinned WHERE a marker may appear and never pinned WHAT A
MARKER IS. How much whitespace around `fb:`, whether a line may carry two markers, what may
follow `-->`. A defect in every implementation is evidence about the specification.

THE RULING. Trailing text after `-->` means the line is PROSE and declares nothing.

That is glm's and codex-luna's reading and I am adopting it after the fact, which I note
plainly because ruling post hoc on an axis the field split on is the thing that most easily
launders a preference into a verdict. The reason is not that two arms chose it. It is that
clauses 6 and 7 exist for exactly one purpose -- stopping a prose EXAMPLE of a marker from
being read as a live declaration -- and wave60 proved the cost of getting that wrong when this
very spec's own example line refused all four arms. A line reading

    <!-- fb:creates x.rs -->   must NOT exist on the base

is prose with a marker in it. The permissive reading parses it as a declaration, which is the
live shell's behaviour and the bug the task was written to fix.

Every cross-camp failure is therefore VETOED and does not count against glm or codex-luna, per
the partition rule. or-nemotron-ultra's survival=1.00 is real but measures robustness across
both readings, not correctness under the ruling one.

DECISION, with the ambiguous axis removed. glm on 24 tests to gemini's 26, because all 24 sit
on a pinned clause or boundary and five of gemini's do not -- CRLF, unclosed comments, plain
HTML comments, a large slice, and the trailing-text case itself. The rubric is explicit that a
suite may not assert outside the spec, precisely because it can fail a correct rival, and
gemini's did, twice. glm also carries the most documentation in the field (87 lines to 62 and
41) and wrote the sharpest critique in it.

Said fairly: gemini's out-of-spec tests are what EXPOSED the gap. The rubric penalty and the
value are both real, and the finding is why crossx exists. On its first dispatch as an
implementer arm it produced the only discriminating suite in a four-arm field.

or-nemotron-ultra deserves its own line: 310 lines and 20 tests in 108 seconds, a third of
glm's wallclock, on an arm that had been parked on a fabricated reset for 29 hours. It is
already on the Pareto frontier at $0.0000.

